{"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00783","type":"uri"},"mass":{"value":"272.1776","type":"literal"},"transform":{"value":"Exogenous estrogens are metabolized using the same mechanism as endogenous estrogens. Estrogens are partially metabolized by cytochrome P450.","type":"literal"}},
{"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01240","type":"uri"},"mass":{"value":"352.225","type":"literal"},"transform":{"value":"Epoprostenol is metabolized to 2 primary metabolites: 6-keto-PGF1&alpha; (formed by spontaneous degradation) and 6,15-diketo-13,14-dihydro-PGF1&alpha; (enzymatically formed), both of which have pharmacological activity orders of magnitude less than epoprostenol in animal test systems. Fourteen additional minor metabolites have been isolated from urine, indicating that epoprostenol is extensively metabolized in humans.","type":"literal"}},
{"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00945","type":"uri"},"mass":{"value":"180.0423","type":"literal"},"transform":{"value":"Aspirin is rapidly hydrolyzed primarily in the liver to salicylic acid, which is conjugated with glycine (forming salicyluric acid) and glucuronic acid and excreted largely in the urine.","type":"literal"}},
{"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00907","type":"uri"},"mass":{"value":"303.1471","type":"literal"},"transform":{"value":"Hepatic. Cocaine is metabolized to benzoylecgonine and ecgonine methyl ester, which are both excreted in the urine. In the presence of alcohol, a further active metabolite, cocaethylene is formed, and is more toxic then cocaine itself.","type":"literal"}},
{"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00572","type":"uri"},"mass":{"value":"289.1678","type":"literal"},"transform":{"value":"Much of the drug is destroyed by enzymatic hydrolysis, particularly in the liver. From 13 to 50% is excreted unchanged in the urine.","type":"literal"}},
