{"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01097","type":"uri"},"mass":{"value":"270.0616","type":"literal"},"transform":{"value":"Primarily hepatic. Leflunomide is converted to its active form following oral intake.","type":"literal"}},
{"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00688","type":"uri"},"mass":{"value":"433.2101","type":"literal"},"transform":{"value":"Following oral and intravenous dosing, mycophenolate mofetil undergoes complete metabolism to MPA, the active metabolite. Metabolism to MPA occurs presystemically after oral dosing. MPA is metabolized principally by glucuronyl transferase to form the phenolic glucuronide of MPA (MPAG) which is not pharmacologically active. In vivo, MPAG is converted to MPA via enterohepatic recirculation. The following metabolites of the 2-hydroxyethyl-morpholino moiety are also recovered in the urine following oral administration of mycophenolate mofetil to healthy subjects: N-(2-carboxymethyl)-morpholine, N-(2-hydroxyethyl)-morpholine, and the N-oxide of N-(2-hydroxyethyl)-morpholine.","type":"literal"}},
{"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00877","type":"uri"},"mass":{"value":"913.5551","type":"literal"}},
{"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01041","type":"uri"},"mass":{"value":"258.0641","type":"literal"},"transform":{"value":"Thalidomide itself does not appear to be hepatically metabolized to any large extent, but appears to undergo non-enzymatic hydrolysis in plasma to multiple metabolites. Thalidomide may be metabolized hepatically by enzymes of the cytochrome P450 enzyme system. The end product of metabolism, phthalic acid, is excreted as a glycine conjugate.","type":"literal"}},
{"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00565","type":"uri"},"mass":{"value":"1242.5004","type":"literal"},"transform":{"value":"The degradation of cisatracurium is largely independent of liver metabolism. Results from in vitro experiments suggest that cisatracurium undergoes Hofmann elimination (a pH and temperature-dependent chemical process) to form laudanosine and the monoquaternary acrylate metabolite. The monoquaternary acrylate undergoes hydrolysis by non-specific plasma esterases to form the monoquaternary alcohol metabolite. The monoquaternary alcohol metabolite can also undergo Hofmann elimination but at a much slower rate than cisatracurium. Laudanosine is further metabolized to desmethyl metabolites which are conjugated with glucuronic acid and excreted in the urine.","type":"literal"}},
{"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01135","type":"uri"},"mass":{"value":"1104.4728","type":"literal"},"transform":{"value":"In vivo data from humans suggest that doxacurium chloride is not metabolized and that the major elimination pathway is excretion of unchanged drug in urine and bile.","type":"literal"}},
{"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00416","type":"uri"},"mass":{"value":"906.1602","type":"literal"}},
{"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00765","type":"uri"},"mass":{"value":"195.0895","type":"literal"},"transform":{"value":"Little biotransformation, with catechol metabolites accounting for less than 1% of the administered dose.","type":"literal"}},
{"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00728","type":"uri"},"mass":{"value":"608.3189","type":"literal"}},
