{"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00356","type":"uri"},"mass":{"value":"168.9931","type":"literal"},"transform":{"value":"Chlorzoxazone is rapidly metabolized in the liver and is excreted in the urine, primarily in a conjugated form as the glucuronide.","type":"literal"}},
{"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00660","type":"uri"},"mass":{"value":"221.1052","type":"literal"},"transform":{"value":"Probably hepatic.","type":"literal"}},
{"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00740","type":"uri"},"mass":{"value":"234.0075","type":"literal"},"transform":{"value":"Riluzole is extensively metabolized to six major and a number of minor metabolites, which have not all been identified to date. Metabolism is mostly hepatic, consisting of cytochrome P450&ndash;dependent hydroxylation and glucuronidation. CYP1A2 is the primary isozyme involved in N-hydroxylation; CYP2D6, CYP2C19, CYP3A4, and CYP2E1 are considered unlikely to contribute significantly to riluzole metabolism in humans.","type":"literal"}},
{"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00810","type":"uri"},"mass":{"value":"311.2249","type":"literal"},"transform":{"value":"The metabolism of biperiden is not completely understood, but does involve hydroxylation.","type":"literal"}},
{"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00494","type":"uri"},"mass":{"value":"305.1012","type":"literal"},"transform":{"value":"Metabolized via isomerization to the cis-isomer, followed by direct glucuronidation of the parent and cis-isomer.","type":"literal"}},
{"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00323","type":"uri"},"mass":{"value":"273.0637","type":"literal"},"transform":{"value":"The main metabolic pathway of tolcapone is glucuronidation","type":"literal"}},
