{"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00667","type":"uri"},"mass":{"value":"307.0334","type":"literal"},"transform":{"value":"Primarily hepatic. Histamine is rapidly metabolized by methylation and oxidation. Methylation involves ring methylation and catalyzation by the enzyme histamine-N-methyltransferase, producing N-methylhistamine, which is mostly converted to N-methyl imidazole acetic acid. 2 to 3% excreted as free histamine, 4 to 8% as N-methylhistamine, 42 to 47% as N-methyl imidazole acetic acid, 9 to 11% as imidazole acetic acid, and 16 to 23% as imidazole acetic acid riboside","type":"literal"}},
{"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00046","type":"uri"},"mass":{"value":"5803.6376","type":"literal"},"transform":{"value":"Insulin is predominantly cleared by metabolic degradation via a receptor-mediated process.","type":"literal"}},
