{"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00480","type":"uri"},"mass":{"value":"259.0957","type":"literal"},"transform":{"value":"The metabolic profile of lenalidomide in humans has not been studied. In healthy volunteers, approximately two-thirds of lenalidomide is eliminated unchanged through urinary excretion. The process exceeds the glomerular filtration rate and therefore is partially or entirely active.","type":"literal"}},
{"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01227","type":"uri"},"mass":{"value":"353.2355","type":"literal"},"transform":{"value":"Levomethadyl acetate undergoes extensive first-pass metabolism to the active demethylated metabolite nor-levomethadyl acetate, which is further demethylated to a second active metabolite, dinor-levomethadyl acetate. These metabolites are more potent than the parent drug.","type":"literal"}},
{"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00922","type":"uri"},"mass":{"value":"280.1073","type":"literal"},"transform":{"value":"Complete metabolism, with some active metabolites (OR-1855 and OR-1896) possibly extending the drug's haemodynamic effects.","type":"literal"}},
{"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01046","type":"uri"},"mass":{"value":"390.2218","type":"literal"},"transform":{"value":"The results of both human and animal studies indicate that lubiprostone is rapidly and extensively metabolized by 15-position reduction, &alpha;-chain &beta;-oxidation, and &omega;-chain &omega;-oxidation. These biotransformations are not mediated by the hepatic cytochrome P450 system but rather appear to be mediated by the ubiquitously expressed carbonyl reductase. M3, a metabolite of lubiprostone in both humans and animals is formed by the reduction of the carbonyl group at the 15-hydroxy moiety that consists of both &alpha;-hydroxy and &beta;-hydroxy epimers. M3 makes up less than 10% of the dose of radiolabeled lubiprostone.","type":"literal"}},
{"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01033","type":"uri"},"mass":{"value":"152.0157","type":"literal"},"transform":{"value":"Hepatic. Degradation primarily by xanthine oxidase. The catabolism of mercaptopurine and its metabolites is complex. In humans, after oral administration of <sup>35</sup>S-6-mercaptopurine, urine contains intact mercaptopurine, thiouric acid (formed by direct oxidation by xanthine oxidase, probably via 6-mercapto-8-hydroxypurine), and a number of 6-methylated thiopurines. The methylthiopurines yield appreciable amounts of inorganic sulfate.","type":"literal"}},
{"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00331","type":"uri"},"mass":{"value":"129.1014","type":"literal"},"transform":{"value":"Metformin is not metabolized.","type":"literal"}},
{"drug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01433","type":"uri"},"mass":{"value":"353.2355","type":"literal"}},
